<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epimenides</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epimenides of Knossos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epimenides was a semi-legendary Cretan seer and poet, traditionally dated to the 7th or 6th century BCE. Greek tradition says he purified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of the pollution caused by the killing of the followers of Cylon, and stories told of his sleep of fifty-seven years in a cave. A line attributed to him, that Cretans are always liars, is quoted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> and gave rise to the liar paradox.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epimenides.jpg" alt="Epimenides" width="256" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epimenides.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
